# gen-go.aon --- Go structs from model.aon.
#
# The shape every generator here uses, and why:
#
#   [&: {...}] & $.records     a LIST spread, because list order is
#                              SOURCE order. `pack` keys by data and
#                              map keys sort by code point, so
#                              pack-then-pick would emit the records
#                              alphabetically -- silently wrong output
#                              for a file.
#
#   rows: [&: ...] & .fields   the per-field lines, STAGED into a key
#                              of their own. `pick` over an inline
#                              spread expression does not resolve (it
#                              stays a symbolic call); staging it into
#                              a named sibling makes it settle.
#
#   body: join(pick(.rows, out), ...)   the projection, in source
#                              order, folded into one string.
#
# The source record's keys ride through the meet -- `name`, `fields`
# and the rest appear beside `head`/`body`/`tail` in the output. That
# is not sloppiness: a spread MEETS, and the meet is exactly what
# makes `.name` and `.fields` resolvable inside the template. A
# REPLACING combinator would lose the reference (G9, "Two specialist
# disagreements, resolved").
#
# `join` LANDED (G9 phase 2), AND THIS CASE CHANGED SHAPE FOR IT.
# Until it did, every LINE was computed by the unifier and the file was
# assembled by six lines of Python in check.sh, because putting a
# separator BETWEEN N items is a fold and the language had none. Now
# `file` below is the whole artifact, one string, computed end to end.
# The host does not assemble anything; check.sh writes bytes.
#
# `rows` IS NOT HIDDEN, AND THAT IS STILL DELIBERATE. The obvious
# spelling wraps the staged intermediate in `hide()` so the scaffolding
# stays out of the evaluated value. It does not survive the parity
# probe: inside `hide()` the Go port never resolves the spread
# templates `.n`, so the transform generates in TypeScript and refuses
# in Go with `mapval_no_gen` -- opposite exit codes on the same
# document (use-cases/BUGS.md 63, still open). Left unhidden, both
# ports agree byte for byte, which check.sh asserts.
@"./model.aon"

units: [&: {
  head: `type ` + .name + ` struct {`
  rows: [&: {
    out: `\t` + .go + ` ` +
         match(.t, "string", `string`, "integer", `int64`) +
         ` \`json:"` + .n + `"\``
  }] & .fields
  body: join(pick(.rows, out), `\n`)
  tail: `}`
  text: .head + `\n` + .body + `\n` + .tail
}] & $.records

# THE WHOLE FILE, one string. Two blank-line-separated units, and the
# trailing newline every text file ends with.
file: join(pick($.units, text), `\n\n`) + `\n`
